    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2003948746657222943",
      "url": "https://x.com/wabenews/status/2003948746657222943",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/wabenews/status/2003948746657222943",
      "text": "Alma Bowman walked out of the ICE field office on Ted Turner Drive just before Thanksgiving with a big smile. \n\n“My heart is just like going boom, boom, boom right now,” she said. “I’m overwhelmed.”\n\nBowman had walked into the same building eight months earlier, only to find herself loaded onto a bus and driven to the Stewart Detention Facility in Lumpkin, where she spent the next eight months locked up.\n\nBorn in the Philippines to a Filipino mother and an American father, Bowman moved to Georgia when she was 10 and has documents showing her claims to American citizenship. \n\nStill, following a traffic stop in 2017, she spent nearly three years in immigrant detention. She was released in 2020, but was ordered to attend regular check-ins with Immigration and Customs Enforcement.\n\nIt was at one of those appointments, on March 26 this year, that she was detained.",
